# Unit files in deploy/systemd/ that ansible neither installs from here
# nor templates a rival for in the archival-node role.
#
# Declaring one is a deliberate act. r1-deployment-state.md documents an
# operator convention of scp-ing units straight out of this directory, so
# an undeclared orphan is a live footgun: it looks like a deployed unit,
# it will start if copied, and nothing will ever reconcile it.
# scripts/ci/lint-deploy-systemd-authority.sh fails on any orphan absent
# from this file (wave-D LID-7).
#
# Format: <unit>  # <why it is kept, and what would retire it>

# The ClickHouse Tier-1 lake's only self-healer. The SCRIPT is real and
# tested (scripts/ops/ch-live-catchup.sh, covered by ch-ops-user-test),
# but nothing in configs/ansible installs the script or these units — so
# on a playbook-provisioned host the healer is simply absent, and an
# unhealed hole permanently stalls the CH-fed projector (resolveTip
# clamps to ContiguousWatermark). Tracked as wave-D LID-1; retire these
# entries by wiring the role to install them, not by deleting the units.
ch-live-catchup.service
ch-live-catchup.timer

# Superseded, not deployed. ExecStart is
# /usr/local/bin/completeness-incremental.sh, which does not exist
# anywhere in this repo — the role ships compute-completeness.{service,
# timer}.j2 for the same job instead. Kept only so the supersession is
# visible rather than silently deleted; safe to remove once an operator
# confirms no host still carries a hand-scp'd copy.
stellarindex-completeness.service
stellarindex-completeness.timer
